{% comment %}
  The panel's height in CSS pixels, which is the unit the Content Limiter
  measures in.

  `trmnl.device.height` reports the panel's **device** pixels: 480 on an OG,
  1404 on a TRMNL X. The framework's `--screen-h` is **CSS** pixels — 480 and
  780 — and the gap between them is the device's pixel ratio, 1 and 1.8.

  That distinction is invisible on an OG, where the two are equal, and it was
  what pushed a card past its column on an X: a budget of 450 was read as 450
  CSS px, which is 16 rows of a ~27px row rather than the 15 intended, so the
  top card overran the half-column and clipped the one beneath it.

  A lookup rather than arithmetic, because no Liquid variable carries the
  pixel ratio — `trmnl.device` exposes `friendly_id`, `width`, `height`,
  `orientation`, `percent_charged` and `wifi_strength`, and nothing else. An
  unrecognised height is taken at face value, which is right for any device
  whose ratio is 1 and errs towards fewer rows rather than an overrun on one
  that scales.
{% endcomment %}
{% comment %}
  `row_pitch` is the tallest thing in a row plus its padding and margin,
  which the row styles express in `em` — so it follows the base font size
  and is a different number of CSS pixels on each device.

  A single constant is therefore wrong on one device by construction. At
  30 on an X the budget bought four fewer rows than it paid for, and cards
  stopped two thirds of the way down their half-column.

  The X's 39 is the OG's 30 taken up by the ratio of their faces. Both
  devices draw the rows at `--device-ui-scale: 1`; what differs is which
  bitmap strike the row's text sits on — TRMNL16 at 16px on an OG,
  TRMNL21 at 21px on an X, which the layouts select per device. The row
  styles are `em`, so the pitch follows: 30 * 21/16 = 39.

  The strike and the pitch are one tuning. Promote a row to a different
  face without moving this and the budget divides the panel by a row
  height the rows no longer have, which overruns a card past its column.

  Both are per-device, so they live together: a new device is one `when`
  with its two numbers.
{% endcomment %}
{% comment %}
  `full_pct` and `split_pct` are the share of the panel a card's rows may
  take: `full_pct` for a card alone in its column, `split_pct` for one of
  two stacked in it.

  They are per-device for the same reason `row_pitch` is. The share is
  what is left after the chrome, and the chrome does not scale with the
  panel: the title bar is 40px on both devices and a card heading is one
  line of the device's own title face. So a bigger panel spends a smaller
  fraction on chrome and can hand more of itself to rows — 72% of an OG
  against 85% of an X.

  Derived from the column rather than tuned by eye. The X's layout box is
  780 - 2*10 gap - 40 title bar = 720px. A card spends 26px on its heading
  (21px title face at 1.2 line-height) and 7px on the gap below it, so a
  lone card fits floor((720 - 33) / 39) = 17 rows, and two stacked fit
  floor(((720 - 7) / 2 - 33) / 39) = 8 each. 17 * 39 = 663, which is 85%
  of 780; 8 * 39 = 312, which is 40%.

  The OG's 72/34 are left as they are. The same arithmetic allows it more
  than it takes, and its numbers are the ones verified on hardware —
  raising them is a separate change needing its own panel to check.
{% endcomment %}
{% assign device_height = trmnl.device.height | default: 480 %}
{% case device_height %}
  {% when 1404 %}
    {% assign panel_height = 780 %}
    {% assign row_pitch = 39 %}
    {% assign full_pct = 85 %}
    {% assign split_pct = 40 %}
  {% else %}
    {% assign panel_height = device_height %}
    {% assign row_pitch = 30 %}
    {% assign full_pct = 72 %}
    {% assign split_pct = 34 %}
{% endcase %}
